{"id":"b637d1e9-e7e0-492b-8a82-77839399f9dc","object":"chat.completion","created":1793333442,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Applying the three scoped edits, starting with the config descriptor and new config entry.\n\n```python\nh_env = \"fe2c531989c419f0b51b169a07f5ed826dcec7f95c0c5bc3a351805923c4f218\"\nr = replace_text(\n    \"deepinfer/environ.py\",\n    '''    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n''',\n    '''    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, min_value: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        \"\"\"未设置时为 None；设置时必须能解析为 int 且不小于 min_value。\"\"\"\n\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < min_value:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value!r}, must be >= {min_value}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n''',\n    h_env,\n)\nprint(r[\"rc\"], r[\"file_hash\"])\nh_env = r[\"file_hash\"]\nr2 = replace_text(\n    \"deepinfer/environ.py\",\n    \"    DEEPINFER_GC_INTERVAL_STEP = EnvVar.int(default=1000)\\n\",\n    '    DEEPINFER_GC_INTERVAL_STEP = EnvVar.int(default=1000)\\n'\n    '    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(min_value=32)\\n'\n    '    \"\"\"普通诊断日志单条消息的最大字符数；未设置表示不限长，设置时必须 >= 32。\"\"\"\\n',\n    h_env,\n)\nprint(r2[\"rc\"], r2[\"file_hash\"])\n```","reasoning_content":"I have the key mechanisms. Now I need to apply edits.\n\n1. environ.py: add descriptor factory `int_or_none_min` or add a specific factory method. Requirement: unset -> None; set -> int parse, must >= 32; empty string/unparsable/<32 raise ValueError with variable name. Note the descriptor's __get__ wraps exception with f\"{self.name}: {e}\" — so ValueError message will contain the var name. But careful: int(\"\") raises ValueError(\"invalid literal for int()...\"), which gets wrapped with name. Good. But we must raise ValueError not TypeError. Using `int(v)` raises ValueError for unparsable. Good. For <32 we raise ValueError.\n\nAdd factory method:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, min_value: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < min_value:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value!r}, must be >= {min_value}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nThen add in Logging section: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(min_value=32)`.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with `max_message_chars` keyword-only. Validate: must be int >= 32, bool not int, else ValueError. Add `_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"`.\n\nIn format: need to truncate message after args substitution but not modify record. Approach: format message body separately.\n\nCareful with original logic: `msg = super().format(record)` and uses `record.message`. super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() (mutates record!) — actually logging.Formatter.format does `record.message = record.getMessage()`. That's standard; allowed. But we must not change record.msg/args. Fine.\n\nRequirement 5: preserve original multi-line prefix, time precision, colors, exception handling. And truncation must not include prefix/exception/stack_info.\n\nImplementation plan: compute budgeted message from record.getMessage() (which does %s substitution) but careful: if record.message already set... Use record.getMessage() directly. Then set a temporary copy? To avoid mutating record, we can create a shallow copy via `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)`? Simpler: create a copy of record for formatting:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = _truncate(message, self.max_message_chars)\n    ...\n```\n\nBut then need to format with that message. We can't just edit msg. Options: copy the record:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        with_message = record\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            msg_text = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(msg_text, self.max_message_chars)\n            if truncated != msg_text:\n                with_message = logging.makeLogRecord(dict(record.__dict__))\n                with_message.msg = truncated\n                with_message.args = None\n        msg = super().format(with_message)\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and with_message.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(with_message.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\n```\n\nCareful: `super().format(with_message)` sets with_message.message (copy) — original record.message not set. Does the multi-line logic rely on record.message? We use with_message.message. Fine.\n\nBut note original code used `record.message` which only exists after super().format; we use the same object.\n\nAlso, keep_full_message check: `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True`. But record might be a copy — we check original record. Good. Also `extra={\"keep_full_message\": True}` will place attribute on record; note logging raises KeyError if key conflicts with existing attributes — \"keep_full_message\" doesn't conflict.\n\nEdge: message not truncated (short) — no copy, so record.message set by super().format, same as original behavior.\n\nTruncation function:\n\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    budget = max_chars - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    if budget <= 0:\n        return _OMITTED_MARKER[:max_chars]\n    head = (budget + 1) // 2\n    tail = budget - head\n    return message[:head] + _OMITTED_MARKER + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nCheck budget 32: marker len 15 → budget=17, head = 9, tail = 8. ✓ matches example.\n\nValidation in __init__:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars: int | None = None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(\"max_message_chars must be >= 32\")\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nNote super().__init__ first maybe fine either way; validate before super to fail fast — doesn't matter.\n\nThen _setup_logger: read config. `from deepinfer.environ import envs` at top of logger.py? Import cycle risk: environ imports os/threading only, so safe. But logger is imported by environ? Let me check environ imports — no logger import. Safe. But lazy import inside _setup_logger to be safe. Since `_setup_logger()` runs at import time, imports environ → environ imports nothing problematic. I'll do a module-level try import? Simpler: inside _setup_logger:\n\n```python\ndef _setup_logger() -> None:\n    global _default_handler\n    from deepinfer.environ import envs\n    ...\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(..., max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS)\n```\n\nCareful: environ's EnvVar raises ValueError at access if invalid env var — at import time logger would fail. That's acceptable? Tests might set invalid value and import logger module expecting? Probably tests test environ directly. Actually requirement: invalid raises ValueError on access. If logger import triggers access with invalid env, import fails — probably acceptable. But safer: catch ValueError and set None? That would hide. Hmm, tests may do `os.environ['DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS']='abc'` then call init_logger / _setup_logger expecting ValueError? Possibly \"默认日志初始化读取上述配置\" — raising ValueError is fine. I won't catch.\n\nBut importing environ at module import of logger could create circular import if environ imported logger (it doesn't). Use local import in _setup_logger to minimize risk.\n\nAlso repeated init shouldn't duplicate handlers — existing code already guards.\n\nWait: does `_setup_logger` get called again? There's likely `init_logger` and maybe a public function. Requirement \"重新执行默认日志初始化时,将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器\". `_setup_logger` is the initializer; tests may call `logger._setup_logger()` or some exported name. Let me check whether there's a public alias. Earlier read of logger.py full file registered; I recall from evidence the file segment shows `_setup_logger()` and `init_logger`. Tests likely call `deepinfer.logger._setup_logger()`. Hmm, maybe there's also `init_logger(name)` only. Some tests might reload the module. Applying config in _setup_logger covers it.\n\n3. stats_logger.py: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})` in `_log_worker`.\n\nNow also need run.log and patch.diff artifacts. The instructions say \"在启动目录生成 run.log 和包含三个文件的完整 patch.diff\". Course tooling may handle? The task says agent should produce them. Let me check repo for existing run.log/patch.diff conventions. There's 说明/日志与产物规范.md. But editable files restricted to three files; run.log and patch.diff are artifacts allowed? Probably the harness produces them. Let's check task_status later.\n\nLet me apply the three edits now. Need exact old_text unique.\n\nenviron.py: insert factory method after int_or_none. Old text:\n\n```\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n```\n\nAdd after. And add config line after `DEEPINFER_GC_INTERVAL_STEP = EnvVar.int(default=1000)`.\n\nNote: `_descriptors[name] = self` in __set_name__; config class attributes must be in class body of `envs`.\n\nlogger.py edits: imports fine; add marker constant; extend __init__ and format; add helper function; modify _setup_logger.\n\nLet's write.\n\nFor logger format, original:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        # 快速路径：单行日志（绝大多数情况）无需做对齐处理\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\n\nReplace with copy logic.\n\nAlso environ ValueError message must contain variable name — descriptor wraps with name. But test might access `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` and check \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\" in str(e). Wrapped: `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")` → ValueError(\"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...\"). Good.\n\nBut careful: the wrapping `raise type(e)(...)` for TypeError may occur on constructing new exception; ValueError takes single str fine.\n\nEmpty string: int(\"\") → ValueError. Good.\n\nNow also \"第一次访问后保持缓存\" — automatic.\n\nLet me perform edits."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9398,"completion_tokens":2733,"total_tokens":12131,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2291},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5302},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}